Here's a SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in[],
    output wire data_out[]
);

reg [7:0] data_in_reg[7:0];
reg [7:0] data_out_reg[7:0];

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_in_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        data_out_reg <= 8'b00000000;
    end else
    begin
        data_in_reg <= data_in[];
        data_out_reg <= encode_ldpc(data_in_reg);
    end
end

function [7:0] encode_ldpc(input [7:0] data_in);
    // Implementation of LDPC encoding algorithm goes here
    // Replace this with your desired encoding logic
    return data_in;
endfunction

assign data_out = data_out_reg;

endmodule
```

This module has a clock (clk), reset (reset), input data (data_in[]), and output data (data_out[]). The module initializes the internal registers (data_in_reg and data_out_reg) to all zeros when the reset signal is asserted. Otherwise, it encodes the input data using the `encode_ldpc` function and stores the result in the output register (data_out_reg).

The `encode_ldpc` function is where you would implement your desired LDPC encoding algorithm. For simplicity, this example returns the input data unchanged. You can replace this with your actual encoding logic based on the LDPC code rate and generator matrix.

Note that this is a basic example and may not be suitable for all FPGA applications. You may need to optimize the design for better performance or reduce the resource usage by using a more efficient encoding algorithm or hardware implementation.

