To handle this exception, you can use a try-catch block to catch the InputMismatchException and prompt the user to enter a valid integer. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this exception:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int age = -1;

        while (age < 0) {
            try {
                System.out.print("Enter your age: ");
                age = scanner.nextInt();
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // Consume the invalid input
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Your age is: " + age);
    }
}
```
In this modified code, we use a while loop to keep prompting the user for their age until they enter a valid integer. If the user enters an invalid input, the try-catch block catches the InputMismatchException and prints an error message. It then consumes the invalid input using the `scanner.nextLine()` method to prevent it from causing any further issues.

After the user enters a valid integer, the program proceeds to print the age.

