Sure! Here's an example of natural selection in MATLAB, simulating the evolution of a population of rabbits with two different fur colors (brown and white). We'll assume that brown rabbits have a higher chance of survival because they can blend in with their environment, as you mentioned.

```matlab
% Define parameters
population_size = 100;
mutation_rate = 0.01;
generations = 100;

% Initialize population
rabbits = zeros(population_size, 2);
rabbits(:, 1) = 'Brown';
rabbits(:, 2) = 'White';

% Simulate evolution
for i = 1:generations
    % Evaluate fitness
    fitness = zeros(population_size, 1);
    for j = 1:population_size
        if rabbits(j, 1) == 'Brown'
            fitness(j) = 1;
        else
            fitness(j) = 0;
        end
    end

    % Select parents
    selected_parents = zeros(population_size, 2);
    for j = 1:population_size
        parent_index = randi([1 population_size]);
        selected_parents(j, :) = rabbits(parent_index, :);
    end

    % Create offspring
    offspring = zeros(population_size, 2);
    for j = 1:population_size
        offspring(j, 1) = selected_parents(j, 1);
        offspring(j, 2) = selected_parents(j, 2);
        if rand() < mutation_rate
            offspring(j, 1) = 'White';
        end
    end

    % Update population
    rabbits = offspring;
end

% Print final population
disp('Final population:');
disp(rabbits);
```

In this simulation, we start with a population of 100 rabbits, half of which are brown and half of which are white. We then simulate 100 generations of evolution, where each generation involves evaluating the fitness of each rabbit

