Here's an R code snippet that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
is_all_numeric <- function(x) {
  is_numeric <- function(x) {
    tryCatch({
      as.numeric(x)
      TRUE
    }, error = function(e) FALSE)
  }

  all(sapply(x, is_numeric))
}

# Example usage
input_vector <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", NA, "5")

if (is_all_numeric(input_vector)) {
  print("All non-NA elements in the input vector can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements in the input vector can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

In this example, the `is_all_numeric` function takes a character vector as input and returns a boolean value indicating whether all non-NA elements in the vector can be converted to numeric. The function uses the `tryCatch` function to handle any errors that may occur during the conversion process. If all elements can be converted to numeric, the function returns `TRUE`, otherwise it returns `FALSE`.

The example usage demonstrates how to use the `is_all_numeric` function with a sample input vector. You can replace the `input_vector` variable with your own character vector to check if its non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.

Here's a simple decorator for your Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and returns `false` if it's not set to `true`.

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvDecorator implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv !== 'true') {
      return false;
    }

    return true;
  }
}
```

Now you can use this decorator in your Nest.js controllers or services like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseInterceptors, ClassSerializerInterceptor } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvDecorator } from './env-decorator';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  @Get()
  @UseInterceptors(ClassSerializerInterceptor)
  @EnvDecorator()
  async getData(): Promise<any> {
    // Your code here
  }
}
```

This decorator will only allow the `getData` method to be executed when the NODE_ENV variable is set to `true`. If it's set to anything else, the method will return `false`.

