To solve the issue of planets being too close together, you can modify your `generateRandomPlanets` function to ensure a minimum distance between each planet. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this updated function, we added a `minDistance` parameter that specifies the minimum distance between two planets. Inside the loop, we check if the new planet is too close to any existing planet using the `tooClose` variable. If it is too close, we skip adding it to the `planets` array and try again in the next iteration.

You can call this function with a suitable `minDistance` value, such as 10 or 20, to ensure that the planets are spread out enough. For example:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 20));
```

This will generate five planets with a minimum distance of 20 between them. Adjust the `minDistance` value as needed to achieve the desired spacing for your planets.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (lines that are too short) from a given text:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [widowedText, setWidowedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const lines = widowedText.split('\n');
    const newLines = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
      const line = lines[i].trim();

      if (line.length > 0) {
        newLines.push(line);
      }
    }

    setWidowedText(newLines.join('\n'));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={widowedText} readOnly />
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply wrap your text in the `RemoveWidows` component and click the "Remove Widows" button when you're ready to remove any widows from the text.

To update your .NET 7 application without replacing the current executable, you can use a combination of techniques, including creating a secondary process, using a background worker thread, and leveraging the built-in .NET 7 update functionality. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a secondary process:

   a. Create a new .NET 7 console application (or any other suitable project) that will serve as the secondary process.

   b. In the secondary process, install the NuGet package `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` and add the following code to the `Program.cs` file:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using YourNamespace; // Replace with the namespace of your main application

namespace SecondaryProcess
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            var host = Host.CreateDefaultBuilder()
                .ConfigureServices((hostContext, services) =>
                {
                    services.AddTransient<IYourService, YourService>(); // Replace with the interface and implementation of the service you want to update
                })
                .Build();

            var service = host.Services.GetRequiredService<IYourService>();

            await service.UpdateAsync(); // Call the method to perform the update

            host.Run();
        }
    }
}
```

   c. Replace `YourNamespace` with the actual namespace of your main application.

2. Use a background worker thread:

   a. In your main application, create a background worker thread that will run the secondary process.

   b. Add the following code to the `Program.cs` file:

```csharp
