To achieve this task using PowerShell, you can use the `Get-Acl` cmdlet along with some custom logic to check for SID history matches. Here's a script that should do the job:

```powershell
# Define the domain name and the path to start checking
$domain = "your_domain_name"
$startPath = "C:\path\to\start"

# Function to check if an SID exists in the SIDHistory of a user or group in the domain
function CheckSidInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domainUsers = Get-ADUser -Filter {(SamAccountName -eq $sid)} -Properties MemberOf -Domain $domain
    foreach ($user in $domainUsers) {
        $memberOf = $user.MemberOf
        if ($memberOf -contains $sid) {
            return True
        }
    }
    return False
}

# Recursively check each folder and its subfolders
function CheckFolderACLs($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
            $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
            if (CheckSidInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                Write-Host "ACL for $folderPath references a SID from SIDHistory: $sid"
            }
        }
    }
    foreach ($subFolder in (Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory)) {
        CheckFolderACLs($subFolder.FullName)
    }
}

# Start checking from the specified path
CheckFolderACLs($startPath)
```

Replace `"your_domain_name"` with your actual domain name, and `"C:\path\to\start"` with the starting path you want to check. This script will recursively check all folders and their subfolders in the specified path and print out any ACLs that reference SIDs from the SIDHistory of users or groups in the domain.
